package core

// why says, for an entry, where its stated time comes from: what is counted
// and how many times, in a sentence or two a learner can check against
// `algorithm bench`. Entries() copies it into Entry.Why; TestWhyNamesRealEntries
// keeps every key a real ID.
var why = map[string]string{
	// sorting
	"bubble-sort":          "Each of up to n passes compares every neighboring pair, so about n^2/2 comparisons; a pass with no swap stops early, which makes sorted input one pass, O(n).",
	"cocktail-shaker-sort": "Bubble sort run in both directions: still up to n passes of up to n comparisons, about n^2/2, but small values at the end move faster.",
	"comb-sort":            "Bubble sort with a gap that shrinks by 1.3 each pass: the large gaps move far values in few passes, so random input takes about log n passes of n comparisons.",
	"cycle-sort":           "For each of the n positions it counts the smaller values in the rest, n comparisons each, so n^2; but each value is written at most once.",
	"gnome-sort":           "It steps back after each swap until the value fits, exactly the swaps insertion sort makes, so about n^2/4 on random input and n steps on sorted input.",
	"heap-sort":            "Building the heap is O(n); then n times it moves the largest to the end and sifts the new root down a tree of height log n, so n log n.",
	"insertion-sort":       "Each of the n values is shifted left past every larger value before it: n^2/4 shifts on average, none on sorted input, so O(n) when sorted.",
	"intro-sort":           "Quick sort, but when the recursion gets deeper than 2 log n it switches to heap sort, so the O(n^2) worst case cannot happen and n log n is guaranteed.",
	"merge-sort":           "Halving gives log n levels, and merging the halves at each level touches all n values once, so n log n whatever the order.",
	"odd-even-sort":        "Alternating passes over the odd and the even pairs; n passes of n/2 comparisons each, so about n^2/2, but every comparison in a pass is independent.",
	"pancake-sort":         "For each size from n down, it finds the largest (up to n comparisons) and flips it to the top and then into place, so n^2 comparisons and at most 2n - 3 flips.",
	"quick-sort":           "Each partition compares every value with the pivot once; good pivots halve the range, giving log n levels of n work, but a pivot that is always the smallest gives n levels, n^2.",
	"selection-sort":       "For each of the n positions it scans the rest for the minimum: n + (n-1) + ... + 1, about n^2/2 comparisons, whatever the input.",
	"shell-sort":           "Insertion sort over shrinking gaps: early passes move values far for little work, so the last pass (gap 1) has little left to do; with these gaps about n^(4/3).",
	"tim-sort":             "It finds runs that are already sorted and merges them like merge sort, so n log n in general and a single n-long pass when the input is already one run.",
	"tree-sort":            "Each of the n inserts walks down a binary search tree of height log n on random input; sorted input makes the tree a line of height n, so n^2.",
	"bitonic-sort":         "A sorting network: log n stages, stage k with k rounds of n/2 compare-exchanges, so n log^2 n comparisons, all known in advance.",
	"stooge-sort":          "It sorts the first two thirds, the last two thirds, then the first two thirds again: T(n) = 3T(2n/3), which solves to n^(log 3 / log 1.5), about n^2.71.",
	"strand-sort":          "Each pass pulls one increasing strand out of the rest (up to n comparisons) and merges it in; random input has about sqrt n strands, the worst case n.",
	"bucket-sort":          "Spreading n values over n buckets is one pass; if they spread evenly each bucket holds a few and sorts in O(1), but all in one bucket is an n^2 insertion sort.",
	"counting-sort":        "One pass counts each value, one pass over the k possible values turns counts into positions, one pass places the values: n + k, with no comparisons.",
	"radix-sort":           "One stable counting pass per digit, each touching all n values, so n times the number of digits k; for numbers below n^c that is O(n) passes of a constant count.",
	"bogo-sort":            "A random shuffle is sorted with probability 1/n!, so it takes n! shuffles on average, each with an n check: hopeless beyond about 10 values.",

	// searching
	"binary-search":        "Each comparison halves the range still possible, and n can be halved only log2 n times before one value is left.",
	"linear-search":        "It looks at the values one by one, so a target at position i costs i comparisons and an absent one costs n.",
	"jump-search":          "It jumps sqrt n steps at a time until it passes the target (at most sqrt n jumps), then walks back at most sqrt n steps, so about 2 sqrt n.",
	"exponential-search":   "It doubles a bound 1, 2, 4, ... until it passes the target at i (log i steps), then binary searches inside that bound (log i more).",
	"interpolation-search": "It guesses the position from the value, like opening a dictionary near the right letter; on evenly spread values each guess shrinks the range to about its square root, giving log log n.",
	"fibonacci-search":     "Like binary search but it splits at Fibonacci numbers instead of halves, so the range shrinks by about 1.618 each step: still log n steps, using only addition.",
	"ternary-search":       "Each step compares two points and keeps two thirds of the range, so the range shrinks by 3/2 per step: log n steps, more comparisons per step than binary search.",
	"lower-bound":          "A binary search for the first value not less than the target: log n halvings, and it finds the boundary even when the target is absent.",
	"upper-bound":          "A binary search for the first value greater than the target: log n halvings.",
	"quickselect":          "Like quick sort but it recurses into only one side: n + n/2 + n/4 + ... is about 2n on average, while bad pivots give n + (n-1) + ..., n^2.",
	"median-of-medians":    "Medians of groups of five give a pivot that is always in the middle 40%, so each step throws away at least 30% of the values: T(n) = T(n/5) + T(7n/10) + n, which is O(n).",

	// pathfinding
	"breadth-first-search": "Every open cell enters the queue at most once and is expanded once, looking at its four neighbors, so the work is a constant times the V cells.",
	"depth-first-search":   "Every cell is pushed and expanded at most once, so O(V); it often finds the end before visiting all of them, but its path need not be the shortest.",
	"dijkstra":             "Each of the V cells is popped from a binary heap once (log V each) and pushes each neighbor at most once (log V each), so about V log V on a grid.",
	"a-star":               "Dijkstra ordered by cost so far plus a distance estimate: the same V log V bound, but a good estimate expands far fewer cells, close to the path's length on an open grid.",
	"greedy-best-first":    "It always expands the cell that looks closest to the end, using a heap (log V per step); fast when the estimate is right, but its path can be longer than the best.",

	// graphs
	"graph-bfs":               "Each of the V nodes is queued once and each of the E edges is looked at once from each end, so V + E.",
	"graph-dfs":               "Each node is visited once and each edge is followed at most twice, so V + E.",
	"graph-dijkstra":          "Each of the V nodes leaves the heap once and each of the E edges may push a node into it, and a heap operation is log V, so (V + E) log V.",
	"a-star-graph":            "Dijkstra's heap work, (V + E) log V at worst, but nodes far from the goal by the estimate are never expanded.",
	"uniform-cost-search":     "Dijkstra that stops at the goal: the same (V + E) log V heap work, often much less.",
	"bellman-ford":            "It relaxes all E edges, V - 1 times, because a shortest path has at most V - 1 edges; one more round finds a negative cycle.",
	"floyd-warshall":          "For each of the V possible middle nodes it updates all V^2 pairs, so V^3, whatever the number of edges.",
	"johnson":                 "One Bellman-Ford (V * E) to remove negative weights, then a Dijkstra from each of the V nodes, (V + E) log V each.",
	"kruskal":                 "Sorting the E edges costs E log E; then each edge is a near-constant union-find check, so the sort dominates.",
	"prim":                    "Each of the E edges may push its far end into a heap of at most E entries, log V each, so E log V.",
	"boruvka":                 "Each round every component picks its cheapest edge (E work) and at least halves the number of components, so log V rounds.",
	"topological-sort":        "Each node is output once when its count of incoming edges reaches zero, and each edge lowers one count once, so V + E.",
	"tarjan-scc":              "One depth-first search, with each node pushed and popped once and each edge looked at once, so V + E.",
	"kosaraju-scc":            "Two depth-first searches, one on the graph and one on its reverse, V + E each.",
	"articulation-points":     "One depth-first search that tracks the lowest node each subtree reaches back to: V + E.",
	"bridges":                 "The same depth-first search as articulation points, checking edges instead of nodes: V + E.",
	"cycle-detection":         "A depth-first search that stops at the first edge back to a node still on the stack: V + E at most.",
	"bipartite-check":         "A breadth-first search coloring each node opposite to its parent: V + E, stopping at the first edge between equal colors.",
	"euler-path":              "Hierholzer's algorithm follows every edge exactly once, splicing cycles together, so V + E.",
	"critical-path":           "A topological order (V + E), then one pass over the edges in that order for the longest path: V + E.",
	"dag-shortest-paths":      "In topological order every edge is relaxed exactly once, so V + E: no heap is needed without cycles.",
	"k-core":                  "Each node is removed at most once and each removal lowers its neighbors' degrees once per edge, so V + E.",
	"max-flow":                "Edmonds-Karp: each augmenting path is a breadth-first search (E), and shortest paths mean at most V * E of them, so V * E^2.",
	"dinic":                   "Each phase builds a level graph with a breadth-first search and pushes a blocking flow; there are at most V phases of V * E work each.",
	"hopcroft-karp":           "Each phase finds a maximal set of shortest augmenting paths in E work, and only about sqrt V phases are needed.",
	"pagerank":                "Each of the k iterations spreads every node's rank along its edges once, so k * (V + E).",
	"stoer-wagner":            "V - 1 phases, each growing a set node by node and merging the last two, with V^2 work per phase on a matrix.",
	"transitive-closure":      "Warshall's algorithm: for each middle node it updates every pair, V^3.",
	"tsp-held-karp":           "It keeps the best path for every subset of the nodes (2^n) ending at every node (n), each found from n smaller ones: 2^n * n^2.",
	"tsp-nearest-neighbor":    "Each of the n steps scans the unvisited nodes for the nearest, so n^2, but the tour can be far from the best.",
	"hamiltonian-path":        "It tries orderings of the nodes with backtracking, and there are up to n! of them; pruning helps but not in the worst case.",
	"bidirectional-bfs":       "Two searches of depth d/2 meeting in the middle: with branching factor b, b^(d/2) twice instead of b^d once.",
	"iterative-deepening-dfs": "Depth-first searches with limits 1, 2, ..., d: the last one dominates with b^d nodes, while memory stays at the depth.",

	// dynamic programming
	"edit-distance":                   "It fills a table with a cell for every prefix pair, (n+1)(m+1) cells, each from three neighbors in constant time.",
	"longest-common-subsequence":      "One cell per pair of prefixes, n * m cells, each from its left, upper or diagonal neighbor.",
	"coin-change":                     "For each amount up to the target it tries every coin, so amount * coins steps.",
	"knapsack-01":                     "One cell per item and capacity, n * capacity, each choosing between taking the item or not; the capacity is a number, so this is pseudo-polynomial.",
	"longest-increasing-subsequence":  "For each of the n values it checks every earlier value, n^2/2 comparisons.",
	"lis-fast":                        "It keeps the smallest tail of each length and binary searches where each value goes: n values, log n each.",
	"longest-palindromic-subsequence": "One cell per substring, n^2/2 of them, each from two smaller ones.",
	"matrix-chain":                    "For each of the n^2/2 sub-chains it tries every split point, so n^3.",
	"rod-cutting":                     "For each length up to n it tries every first cut, 1 + 2 + ... + n, about n^2/2.",
	"subset-sum":                      "One cell per value and partial sum up to the target, n * target.",
	"unique-paths":                    "Each cell is the sum of the cells above and to the left, so one constant step per cell, rows * cols.",
	"egg-drop":                        "For each number of eggs it grows the number of drops until the floors covered reach the building's height, eggs * drops cells.",

	// string matching and text
	"naive-search":         "At each of the n positions it compares up to m characters of the pattern, so n * m in the worst case (text aaaa..., pattern aaab).",
	"knuth-morris-pratt":   "The failure table (m) lets it never move back in the text, so each of the n characters is compared a bounded number of times: n + m.",
	"rabin-karp":           "A rolling hash updates in constant time per shift, so n + m, plus m for each position whose hash matches but whose text does not.",
	"z-algorithm":          "The Z box it maintains means each character is compared again only to extend the box, so n + m in total.",
	"boyer-moore-horspool": "It compares from the pattern's end and can skip m characters on a mismatch, so about n / m on ordinary text, but n * m on repetitive text.",
	"aho-corasick":         "A trie of all the patterns with failure links is walked once over the text, one step per character, plus one report per match.",
	"manacher":             "It reuses the mirror of each palindrome it has found, so its right edge only moves forward: about 2n steps.",
	"suffix-array":         "Prefix doubling: log n rounds, each sorting the n suffixes by pairs of ranks, n log n per round with a comparison sort, so n log^2 n.",
	"anagram":              "One pass counting each letter of both words, then one comparison of the 26 counts.",

	// recursion and numbers
	"factorial":               "One multiplication per number from 1 to n.",
	"fibonacci":               "F(n) calls F(n-1) and F(n-2), which repeat the same work, so the calls grow like F(n) itself, about 1.618^n.",
	"fibonacci-memo":          "Each F(k) is computed once and remembered, so n additions instead of 1.618^n calls.",
	"tower-of-hanoi":          "Moving n disks means moving n-1, one disk, then n-1 again: 2^n - 1 moves, and no solution has fewer.",
	"permutations":            "There are n! orderings and writing each one out takes n steps.",
	"gcd":                     "Each step replaces (a, b) by (b, a mod b), and every two steps at least halve the smaller number, so about log of the smaller.",
	"lcm":                     "lcm(a, b) = a / gcd(a, b) * b, so it costs one gcd.",
	"ackermann":               "Its recursion grows faster than any tower of exponents: A(4, 2) has 19,729 digits, so only tiny inputs finish.",
	"palindrome":              "It compares the outer pair of characters and recurses inward, n/2 comparisons.",
	"prime":                   "A composite n has a factor at most sqrt n, so trial division only goes up to sqrt n.",
	"prime-factors":           "Trial division up to sqrt n, dividing out each factor as it is found.",
	"divisors":                "Divisors come in pairs d and n/d with one of each pair at most sqrt n, so it tests up to sqrt n.",
	"sieve-of-eratosthenes":   "Each prime p crosses out n/p multiples, and n/2 + n/3 + n/5 + ... over the primes adds up to n log log n.",
	"fast-power":              "Squaring halves the exponent at each step, so log exp multiplications instead of exp.",
	"fibonacci-matrix":        "F(n) is a power of a 2 x 2 matrix, and fast powering takes log n matrix products.",
	"fibonacci-fast-doubling": "F(2k) and F(2k+1) come from F(k) and F(k+1), so it halves n each step: log n steps.",
	"karatsuba":               "Three half-size products instead of four: T(n) = 3T(n/2) + n, which solves to n^(log2 3), about n^1.585.",
	"euler-totient":           "It factors n by trial division up to sqrt n and applies each prime factor once.",
	"extended-gcd":            "The same steps as Euclid's gcd, carrying the coefficients along.",

	// greedy and backtracking
	"activity-selection":      "Sorting by end time is n log n; one pass then picks every activity that starts after the last one picked.",
	"fractional-knapsack":     "Sorting by value per weight is n log n; one pass then fills the knapsack.",
	"interval-partitioning":   "Sorting by start (n log n), then a heap of room end times, log n per interval.",
	"huffman-coding":          "One pass counts the n symbols; then k - 1 merges of the two rarest of k symbols, log k each with a heap.",
	"n-queens":                "It places one queen per row and backtracks on a clash; without pruning that is n! placements, and pruning cuts most of them.",
	"subset-sum-backtracking": "Each value is in or out, 2^n combinations, cut short when the sum passes the target.",
	"graph-coloring":          "Each of the n nodes tries each of the m colors, m^n assignments at worst before backtracking prunes.",

	// arrays
	"dynamic-array":      "Appends are O(1) amortized: when full it doubles, copying n values, but that happens after n cheap appends, so each pays for about one copy.",
	"max-subarray":       "Kadane's algorithm keeps the best sum ending here and the best overall, updating both once per value: one pass.",
	"prefix-sums":        "One pass builds the running totals; afterward any range sum is one subtraction.",
	"reverse-array":      "Two pointers from the ends swap and meet in the middle: n/2 swaps.",
	"rotate-array":       "Three reversals (the whole array, then each part) touch every value twice.",
	"sliding-window-max": "A deque of candidates: each value is pushed once and popped at most once, so n in total even though each window has k values.",
	"two-pointers":       "On sorted values one pointer moves up or the other moves down at each step, so they meet after at most n steps.",
	"js-sort":            "It is Go's stable sort (slices.SortStableFunc) under a JavaScript-style comparator, n log n comparisons, the same bound JavaScript engines give.",
	"js-map":             "The callback runs once per element.",
	"js-filter":          "The callback runs once per element, and kept elements are copied once.",
	"js-reduce":          "The callback runs once per element, carrying the accumulator.",
	"js-splice":          "Removing or inserting in the middle shifts every element after it.",

	// structures
	"stack":                 "Push and pop work at the top of a slice: no other element moves, so each is O(1) (amortized when the slice grows).",
	"queue":                 "A ring buffer with a head and a tail index: enqueue and dequeue move one index, no element shifts.",
	"deque":                 "A ring buffer that can move its head back or its tail forward, so both ends are O(1).",
	"circular-buffer":       "A fixed array with head and tail indexes that wrap around: one index move per push or pop.",
	"linked-list":           "Adding at the head or the tracked tail relinks a pointer or two; finding a value walks the list, n steps.",
	"doubly-linked-list":    "Each node knows both neighbors, so either end is a few pointer changes; search still walks n nodes.",
	"min-stack":             "Each entry also stores the minimum so far, so the minimum is read from the top in O(1).",
	"monotonic-queue":       "Values that can never be the maximum are dropped as new ones arrive; each value is added and removed once, O(1) amortized.",
	"sorted-list":           "Finding is a binary search, log n; inserting must shift every later value to make room, n.",
	"min-heap":              "A complete binary tree of height log n kept in an array: push sifts up and pop sifts down at most log n levels; the minimum is always the root.",
	"custom-priority-queue": "A binary heap ordered by your priority: log n per push or pop, O(1) to peek.",
	"binary-search-tree":    "Each operation walks one root-to-leaf path, so it costs the height: log n when balanced, n when the inserts came in sorted order.",
	"avl-tree":              "Rotations keep the heights of every node's two subtrees within one, so the height stays below 1.44 log n.",
	"red-black-tree":        "The coloring rules keep the longest path at most twice the shortest, so the height is at most 2 log n.",
	"b-tree":                "Each node holds up to 2t - 1 keys, so the tree is only log_t n high; each level costs a scan of t keys.",
	"skip-list":             "Each level skips about half of the one below, so a search drops through about log n levels, taking a couple of steps on each.",
	"trie":                  "Each operation walks one node per character of the key, so it costs the key's length, not the number of keys stored.",
	"hash-table":            "The hash picks a bucket directly; with a bounded load each bucket holds a few keys on average, so O(1), but keys that all collide make it a list, O(n).",
	"union-find":            "Union by rank keeps the trees shallow and path compression flattens them as they are walked, so each operation is nearly O(1) (inverse Ackermann).",
	"lru-cache":             "A hash map finds the node and a doubly linked list moves it to the front, both O(1).",
	"lfu-cache":             "A hash map per key and a list per frequency, with the minimum frequency tracked, so get and put are O(1).",
	"bloom-filter":          "Adding or checking sets or tests k bits picked by k hashes: O(k), whatever the number of items.",
	"segment-tree":          "A range splits into at most 2 log n tree nodes, and an update changes the log n nodes above one leaf.",
	"fenwick-tree":          "Each index covers a range given by its lowest set bit, so an update or a prefix sum visits log n indexes.",
	"sparse-table":          "It precomputes answers for every power-of-two length (n log n); a minimum query is two overlapping lookups, O(1).",
	"set":                   "A hash map with no values: add, remove and contains hash the key once, O(1) on average.",
	"access-orders":         "The values go through a stack and a queue (O(1) each) and a heap (log n each), so the heap's n log n dominates.",

	// hashing
	"linear-probing":     "A collision tries the next slot; at a bounded load the expected probe sequence is short, but clusters form as it fills.",
	"quadratic-probing":  "A collision jumps 1, 4, 9, ... slots, which breaks up clusters, so probes stay few at a bounded load.",
	"double-hashing":     "A second hash picks the step size, so colliding keys follow different paths and probes stay few.",
	"cuckoo-hashing":     "Each key lives in one of two places, so a lookup checks at most two; inserts may kick keys around but rarely far.",
	"robin-hood-hashing": "Keys far from home take the slot of keys close to theirs, evening out probe lengths, so lookups stay short and predictable.",
	"consistent-hashing": "Nodes sit at sorted points on a ring; a key goes to the next node clockwise, a binary search, log n.",

	// trees
	"tree-inorder":           "Each node is visited once: left subtree, the node, right subtree.",
	"tree-preorder":          "Each node is visited once, before its subtrees.",
	"tree-postorder":         "Each node is visited once, after its subtrees.",
	"tree-level-order":       "A queue holds each node once, level by level.",
	"morris-traversal":       "It threads temporary links to each node's predecessor and removes them, following each edge at most three times, with O(1) extra memory.",
	"validate-bst":           "Each node is checked once against the range its ancestors allow.",
	"tree-height-diameter":   "One postorder pass computes each node's height from its children's.",
	"lowest-common-ancestor": "One pass down the tree; at worst it visits every node once.",

	// sets
	"power-set":           "There are 2^n subsets and writing each takes up to n steps.",
	"sorted-union":        "Two pointers walk both sorted lists once, like merge sort's merge: n + m.",
	"sorted-intersection": "Two pointers advance past the smaller value each step: n + m.",
	"ordered-set":         "Binary search finds a value in log n, but inserting into a sorted slice shifts the later values, n.",
	"bitset":              "A value's bit is found by one division and one mask, O(1); counting adds up n/64 words.",

	// operating systems
	"fcfs-scheduling":          "The simulation advances one tick at a time, and at each of the T ticks it looks over the n processes to see who arrives and who runs.",
	"sjf-scheduling":           "At each of the T ticks the simulation scans the n processes, and when the CPU frees up it picks the shortest ready job from that scan.",
	"srtf-scheduling":          "At each of the T ticks it scans the n processes for the one with the least time left, so a newly arrived short job can preempt.",
	"round-robin":              "At each of the T ticks the simulation updates the n processes; every quantum ticks the running one goes to the back of the queue.",
	"priority-scheduling":      "At each of the T ticks it scans the n processes for the highest priority among those ready.",
	"fifo-page-replacement":    "Each of the n references checks the frames and, on a fault, evicts the oldest: frames per reference.",
	"lru-page-replacement":     "Each reference checks the frames and, on a fault, evicts the least recently used: frames per reference.",
	"optimal-page-replacement": "Each of the n references checks the frames, and on a fault compares when each frame's page is next used to evict the one used furthest in the future: frames per reference.",
	"clock-page-replacement":   "A hand sweeps the frames clearing reference bits until it finds a clear one; at most one full sweep per fault.",
	"first-fit":                "Each process scans the partitions from the start until one is big enough.",
	"best-fit":                 "Each process scans every partition for the smallest that fits.",
	"worst-fit":                "Each process scans every partition for the largest.",
	"fcfs-disk":                "Requests are served in order, one head move each.",
	"sstf-disk":                "Each step scans the pending requests for the nearest to the head, n times n.",
	"scan-disk":                "After sorting the requests (n log n) the head sweeps once to the end and back.",
	"c-scan-disk":              "After sorting, the head sweeps one way, jumps back, and sweeps again.",
	"look-disk":                "Like SCAN but it turns at the last request, not the end of the disk.",
	"c-look-disk":              "Like C-SCAN but it jumps from the last request to the first.",
}
